  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 PM EDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nA cool/cloudy pattern should persist through Wednesday. A cut-off \nupper level low across northern Illinois has been responsible \nfor the bulk of scattered shower/isolated thunderstorm activity \nthis afternoon. It is possible as this mid/upper level wave \nshifts east into this evening a few rain showers/sprinkles could\nmove into NW Indiana. Across the local area, model time/height \nsections do indicate some weak forcing/lift in the 800-700 mb \nlayer likely due to some elevated warm advection forcing \ndownstream of the northern IL short wave trough. Moisture \nprofiles are relatively shallow in nature with indications in \ncross section data that this better lift remains just offset \nvertically above the shallow better low level RH. The result of \nthis lack of juxtaposition has made precip production somewhat \ninefficient today with just some pockets of light drizzle at \ntimes. \n\nThrough time tonight the upper level short wave across IL will \nshift east with some weak phasing of another mid level height \nminimum across NW Ohio. This gradual phasing should support \ncontinued respectable easterly flow and slightly deeper moisture\nprofiles being advected westward into NE IN/NW Ohio overnight \nand especially into Wednesday. Given marginal moisture depths \nlingering this evening into the overnight, some question as to \nextent of any rain given overall weakly forced nature of this \nsynoptic setup and overall preference to weak synoptic scale \nsubsidence. However, could see just enough lift in slightly \ndeepening moisture profiles to promote some pockets of very \nlight rain or drizzle and will likely maintain the 15 PoPs. \nDiurnal ranges for temps should continue to be limited tonight \nby lack of decoupling and the potential of low clouds lingering.\nThis should limit lows from dropping too much, holding in the \n50 to 55 degree range.\n\nHave not made significant changes to the forecast for Wednesday with \ngreater rain chances (20-40%) generally along and east of the \nInterstate 69 corridor. Higher res guidance does suggest presence of \npocket of weak MUCAPE around 500 J/kg working westward into NE IN/NW \nOH given the weak aforementioned phasing of mid level features. The \ndepth of instability could be just enough to realize the weak \nelevated instability. Still some uncertainty persists regarding \ncoverage as despite mid level trough in the vicinity this \nappears to be otherwise a very weakly forced setup. By wednesday\nevening, primary upper low should begin to sink southeast \nallowing any linger showers across northeast IN/northwest OH to \ndrop southeast of the area. Any additional rainfall on Wednesday\nis not expected to significantly impact ongoing river flood \nconcerns across Maumee/Wabash basins.\n\nThe remainder of the work week into much of the weekend will feature \na rather stagnant, blocky pattern as large scale flow begins to \namplify. Temperature advections in this pattern will remain weak \nthrough Sunday which will keep below normal temperatures going into \nSunday. Medium range guidance has indicated a Pacific wave dampening \nthrough the longwave ridge early next week with prospects for precip \nlooking minimal with this wave as moisture should still be on the \nmarginal side. Temperatures should moderate toward the end of this \nperiod as wave progression begins to increase and stronger southwest \nflow develops.",
